---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import SectionNav from "../components/SectionNav.astro";
import { pricing, providerName } from "../lib/data";
import { price, presets, formatMoney } from "../lib/cost";

// The personal-agent workload, priced on every sandbox with the calculator's engine.
const personal = presets.personal.workload;
const sandboxes = pricing
  .map((card) => ({ id: card.provider, q: price(card, personal) }))
  .filter((r) => r.q.feasible)
  .sort((a, b) => a.q.total - b.q.total);
const cheapest = sandboxes.slice(0, 3);
const median = sandboxes[Math.floor(sandboxes.length / 2)];

// Non-sandbox homes for the same 2 vCPU / 4 GB always-on machine. Read 2026-10-29.
const others = [
  {
    kind: "A plain server (VPS)",
    name: "Hetzner Cloud CX23",
    size: "2 vCPU, 4 GB, 40 GB disk, 20 TB traffic",
    month: "€5.99 (about $7), excl. VAT",
    url: "https://www.hetzner.com/cloud/cost-optimized/",
    note: "EU and US locations. The monthly price is a cap; deleting early bills by the hour.",
  },
  {
    kind: "A plain server (VPS)",
    name: "DigitalOcean Basic Droplet",
    size: "2 vCPU, 4 GB, 80 GB disk, 4 TB transfer",
    month: "$24",
    url: "https://www.digitalocean.com/pricing/droplets",
    note: "Smaller sizes from $6 (1 vCPU, 1 GB) and $12 (1 vCPU, 2 GB).",
  },
  {
    kind: "An app platform",
    name: "Fly.io shared-cpu-2x",
    size: "2 shared vCPU, 4 GB, plus a 20 GB volume",
    month: "about $25 ($22.22 machine + $3.00 volume)",
    url: "https://fly.io/docs/about/pricing/",
    note: "Price varies by region, from $21.40 to $34.56 for the machine. OpenClaw's own Fly guide expects $10–15 on 2 GB.",
  },
  {
    kind: "An app platform",
    name: "Railway Hobby",
    size: "Billed on what the service actually uses",
    month: "about $26 if it holds 2 GB and idles (inferred)",
    url: "https://railway.com/pricing",
    note: "$5 minimum includes $5 of usage. Memory about $10 per GB-month, CPU about $20 per vCPU-month while working, volumes $0.16 per GB-month.",
  },
  {
    kind: "An app platform",
    name: "Render",
    size: "1 CPU, 2 GB",
    month: "$25",
    url: "https://render.com/pricing",
    note: "The free tier sleeps after 15 minutes without traffic, which an agent waiting for chat messages cannot use.",
  },
  {
    kind: "Managed one-click",
    name: "Hostinger OpenClaw",
    size: "OpenClaw set up for you",
    month: "$4.99 promotional, renews at $11.99 (24-month term)",
    url: "https://www.hostinger.com/openclaw",
    note: "Only OpenClaw. You pick it from a menu instead of installing it.",
  },
  {
    kind: "Managed one-click",
    name: "Agent 37 Personal",
    size: "Hermes or OpenClaw set up for you",
    month: "from $3.99; $29.99 with a built-in browser",
    url: "https://www.agent37.com/personal",
    note: "Agent 37 is also one of the sandboxes this guide compares. Its free tier sleeps after 15 minutes.",
  },
];

const nav = [
  { href: "#short", label: "Short answer" },
  { href: "#options", label: "Five places to run it" },
  { href: "#prices", label: "Same machine, every home" },
  { href: "#worth-it", label: "When a sandbox is worth it" },
  { href: "#not", label: "When it is not" },
];
---

<Base title="Do you even need a sandbox?" description="A cloud sandbox is one of five places to run an AI agent. What each costs for the same always-on machine, and when a sandbox is worth paying for.">
  <PageHeader
    kicker="Before you choose"
    title="Do you even need a sandbox?"
    standfirst="A cloud sandbox is one of five places an agent can live. For one personal agent that stays on all day, a plain rented server is often cheaper; a sandbox earns its price when you need many machines, throwaway machines, or machines that sleep when nobody is using them."
    meta={["Prices read 2026-10-29", "Same 2 vCPU / 4 GB machine throughout", "Sources linked"]}
  />

  <SectionNav items={nav} />

  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="section section--flush" id="short">
      <div class="section__head"><h2 class="section__title">The short answer</h2></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>One agent for yourself, always on, and you are happy to follow a setup guide:</strong> a plain server (Hetzner, DigitalOcean) is usually the cheapest. You look after it yourself.</li>
          <li><strong>One agent for yourself and you never want to see a terminal:</strong> a managed one-click service (Hostinger, Agent 37). It costs little, but you get only the agents on its menu.</li>
          <li><strong>Many short jobs, code from other people, or agents that should cost nothing when idle:</strong> a cloud sandbox. That is what the rest of this guide compares.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Not sure? <a href="/choose/">Help me choose</a> asks four questions and gives you a short list.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="options">
      <div class="section__head"><h2 class="section__title">Five places to run an agent</h2></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h3>1. Your own computer</h3>
        <p>
          Free, and the quickest way to try an agent. It stops when the computer sleeps or loses its connection, and
          the agent can read and change your own files. Fine for an afternoon, not for something you message from your phone.
        </p>
        <h3>2. A plain rented server (a VPS)</h3>
        <p>
          A whole machine in a data centre at a flat monthly price, running all the time. You connect to it from a
          terminal, install the agent by following its guide, and you keep the system updated and secure yourself. The
          Hermes README describes running it on <a href="https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent" rel="noreferrer noopener">"a $5 VPS"</a>.
          Nothing is sealed off: if the agent misbehaves, it can damage only that server, which is the main thing a sandbox would add.
        </p>
        <h3>3. An app platform</h3>
        <p>
          Services like Fly.io, Railway and Render run a program from a template or a short configuration file and
          keep it running. Less server upkeep than a VPS, more setup than a one-click service. OpenClaw publishes guides
          for <a href="https://docs.openclaw.ai/install/fly" rel="noreferrer noopener">Fly.io</a> and{" "}
          <a href="https://docs.openclaw.ai/install/render" rel="noreferrer noopener">Render</a>.
        </p>
        <h3>4. A managed one-click service</h3>
        <p>
          You sign up, pick the agent from a list and connect your chat app. No terminal. The trade is choice: you get
          the agents and settings the service offers, and moving away later means starting over.
        </p>
        <h3>5. A cloud sandbox</h3>
        <p>
          A sealed-off machine you create from a program or a dashboard, often in under a second, billed by the second,
          and easy to copy, pause or throw away. Built for software that creates machines on demand. See{" "}
          <a href="/features/">what sandboxes offer</a> and <a href="/use-cases/">what people run on them</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="prices">
      <div class="section__head"><h2 class="section__title">The same machine in every kind of home</h2></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          One machine with 2 vCPU and 4 GB of memory that stays on all month, the size this guide uses for a personal
          Hermes or OpenClaw agent. Prices are the published list prices, before tax, read on 2026-10-29. The AI model
          is billed separately and is the same wherever the machine lives.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Kind</th><th scope="col">Option</th><th scope="col">What you get</th><th scope="col">Per month</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {others.map((o) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{o.kind}</td>
                <th scope="row"><a href={o.url} rel="noreferrer noopener">{o.name}</a></th>
                <td>{o.size}<br /><span class="muted">{o.note}</span></td>
                <td>{o.month}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
            {cheapest.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td>A cloud sandbox</td>
                <th scope="row"><a href={`/providers/${r.id}/`}>{providerName(r.id)}</a></th>
                <td>{r.q.shape}{r.q.plan ? ` (plan: ${r.q.plan})` : ""}<br /><span class="muted">Priced by the calculator with 20 GiB kept and about 6% CPU use.</span></td>
                <td>{formatMoney(r.q.total)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Those are the three cheapest of the {sandboxes.length} sandboxes that can run this machine. The middle one,{" "}
          {providerName(median.id)}, comes to {formatMoney(median.q.total)}.{" "}
          <a href="/costs/?preset=personal">Change the numbers in the calculator</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="worth-it">
      <div class="section__head"><h2 class="section__title">When a sandbox is worth paying for</h2></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The agent runs code you did not write</strong>, such as code from your customers or from the model itself, and it must not reach anything else. Sandboxes are built to keep it sealed in.</li>
          <li><strong>You need many machines, briefly.</strong> A thousand ten-minute jobs a month cost a few dollars on a sandbox; a server would sit idle the rest of the time.</li>
          <li><strong>It should cost nothing while nobody uses it.</strong> Several sandboxes sleep when idle and wake on a request, so a part-time agent pays for part-time hours.</li>
          <li><strong>You want to copy, roll back or pause it.</strong> Snapshots and forks let you save a machine exactly as it is and start new ones from that point.</li>
          <li><strong>Your own software creates the machines.</strong> Every sandbox has an API for this; a VPS usually means a person clicking.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="not">
      <div class="section__head"><h2 class="section__title">When it is probably not</h2></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>One personal agent, on all the time, that only you use.</strong> A server at a flat price usually wins on cost, and the isolation a sandbox adds protects little that a separate server does not.</li>
          <li><strong>You want no setup at all.</strong> Most sandboxes expect you to follow developer instructions. A managed one-click service, or Agent 37's one-click route, is closer to what you want.</li>
          <li><strong>A predictable bill matters more than flexibility.</strong> Flat monthly prices are easier to plan around than per-second rates.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The server and app-platform prices on this page were read once, on 2026-10-29. Unlike the sandbox rates,
          they are not checked again with each update, so follow the links before you rely on them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
